// The `geo` namespace (area 20, "Operations"). Each operation is listed,
// typed, in `jadpo-std`'s operation table, which names the Rust function
// in `jadpo-value` that runs it; a test holds these stubs to that table.
//
// The measures are on the WGS84 ellipsoid by C. F. F. Karney's geodesic
// methods, as PostGIS's `geography` measures, in Jadpo's own fixed-point
// code, so every target gives the same digits; altitude is ignored. A
// shape's parameter, `T` or `U`, takes only the geometries the operation
// names: `length` a `LineString` or a `MultiLineString`, `area` a
// `Polygon` or a `MultiPolygon`, and the predicates and shapes any
// geometry or a `Geometry` (chunk 5.5u3), `contains` a `BoundingBox` or a
// `Circle` too. Edges are great circles, and inside includes the edge, as
// PostGIS's `ST_Covers` and `ST_Intersects` on `geography`. WKT's two
// operations (chunk 5.5u6) are the one place outside GeoJSON where a
// position's longitude comes first.

public library geo {
    /// A `Polygon`'s or `MultiPolygon`'s area on the ellipsoid, each edge a
    /// geodesic and each ring the smaller of the two sides it bounds, holes
    /// taken away, rounded half to even to the square millimetre.
    function area(shape: T) -> Area {}

    /// The smallest box of parallels and meridians holding the shape, an
    /// edge's highest or lowest point counted, across the antimeridian
    /// when that box is narrower; a shape holding a pole runs from -180 to
    /// 180.
    function bounding_box(shape: T) -> BoundingBox {}

    /// The direction the geodesic leaves `from` in, in degrees clockwise
    /// from true north, at least 0 and below 360, to 9 places. Two equal
    /// points have none: the call fails with `same_point`, which its
    /// `attempt` maps.
    function bearing(from: Point, to: Point) -> Bearing {}

    /// The shape's centre on the sphere, as PostGIS's `ST_Centroid(x,
    /// false)` finds it: each point a unit vector, weighted by area,
    /// length or count by the shape's kind, summed, and back to latitude
    /// and longitude. It may lie outside a concave polygon.
    function centroid(shape: T) -> Point {}

    /// Whether no point of `b` lies outside `a`, edges included. `a` may
    /// be a `BoundingBox`, whose sides are compared directly, or a
    /// `Circle`, holding the points within its radius of its centre.
    function contains(a: T, b: U) -> Bool {}

    /// The geodesic distance between two points on the ellipsoid, rounded
    /// half to even to the micrometre.
    function distance(from: Point, to: Point) -> Length {}

    /// The geometry WKT or EWKT text writes, longitude first:
    /// `POLYGON((-0.2 51.49,-0.05 51.49,-0.05 51.54,-0.2 51.49))`, any of
    /// the six, with `Z` for an altitude, held as the `Geometry` of its
    /// kind. `SRID=4326;` may lead; another system, or a coordinate out of
    /// its range, fails with `out_of_range`, and text that isn't one of
    /// the six geometries, a polygon whose rings aren't valid included,
    /// with `malformed`, which the call's `attempt` maps.
    function from_wkt(text: Text) -> Geometry {}

    /// Whether the two share at least one point, edges included.
    function intersects(a: T, b: U) -> Bool {}

    /// A `LineString`'s or `MultiLineString`'s length: each edge measured
    /// as `distance` measures it, and summed.
    function length(line: T) -> Length {}

    /// The shape as WKT, longitude first, as PostGIS's `ST_AsText` writes
    /// it: `POINT(-0.1276 51.5072)`, `POINT Z (-0.1276 51.5072 35)` with an
    /// altitude, each ring in the order it holds its points.
    function to_wkt(shape: T) -> Text {}

    /// `geo.contains(b, a)`; or, with `distance:`, whether two points are
    /// at most that far apart, as `geo.distance` measures them.
    function within(a: T, b: U, distance: Length?) -> Bool {}
}
